ENDF-6 Format. Translated from the Livermore ENDL format          1500 1451
to the ENDF-6 Format.                                             1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
  Contents                                                        1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
  MF/MT    Description                                            1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
  28/533   Atomic Relaxation Data for Electrons and Photons       1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
  Warning                                                         1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
  MF/MT combinations used to define ALL electron interaction data 1500 1451
  are newly defined MF/MT numbers for ENDF/B-VI that did not exist1500 1451
  in earlier versions of ENDF/B.                                  1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
  Definition of Data                                              1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
  Atomic Relaxation Data for Electrons and Photons                1500 1451
==================================================================1500 1451
1) An atom can be ionized by a photon or an electron interaction. 1500 1451
2) The atomic relaxation data included here describes how a       1500 1451
   singly ionized atom returns to neutrality, by emitting photons 1500 1451
   (x-rays) and/or electrons (Auger, Coster-Kronig).              1500 1451
3) Data is given to define the transition probabilities between   1500 1451
   subshells for radiative (x-ray emission) and non-radiative     1500 1451
   (Auger, Coster-Kronig) transitions.                            1500 1451
4) The complete spectrum of emitted photons and electrons can be  1500 1451
   calculated using the data included here, e.g., see ref. 2,     1500 1451
   below, for am example of a computer code that analytically     1500 1451
   calculates these spectra.                                      1500 1451
